Subject: A question about parameter estimation and coverage in a Bayesian inference framework
Message-ID: <Y8QnNyksAxs.jHphyRfXDwAJ@hmecology.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/hmecology/c/Y8QnNyksAxs/m/jHphyRfXDwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I've recently finished a simulation where I generated many data sets with known, constant parameter values. I analyzed the simulated data with a method that estimates the data-generating parameters in JAGS. In addition to looking at things like bias, I recorded the proportion of BCIs that contained the true values.  Is this anything like coverage in classical inference?  Can a Bayesian use a frequentist view of probability in this way? BTW, in many simulations, nominal levels were not attained.  I attribute this result to non-asymptotic properties of the small data sets I analyzed, perhaps the use of poor (non-proper) priors, and the most obvious effect of parameter bias.  Any thoughts on this matter would be greatly appreciated.

